# Dated full-sheet restore points (npm run data:snapshot). Committed on purpose:
# the sheet is production and an import replaces it, so the point before an
# import has to be recoverable from a commit, not from someone Downloads folder.
!data/snapshot_*.csv
# A fetched live-sheet export and the import file built from it. Deliberately
# NOT named snapshot_* : that glob means "a dated snapshot of the app dataset",
# and snapshotFidelity.test.ts asserts the newest one matches the shipped 169
# rows field for field. A live export is 171 rows, so filing it under snapshot_*
# made a passing invariant fail on a file that was correct.
#
# THE REASON GIVEN HERE FOR THE TWO EXTRA ROWS WAS WRONG and is corrected on
# 30 August 2026. This said build-dataset "drops for having no coordinates" the
# two rows a live export carries. It does not, and has not since 22 August:
# `isValidRow` keeps a named row with empty coordinates on purpose — the comment
# there names Shah Gohar Peer as the case that motivated the change, and he is
# one of the two. Run over the committed 171-row export, the current predicate
# keeps all 171. The snapshot ships 169 because it PREDATES that fix, not because
# anything rejects them, and `npm run data:build` is the whole of the remedy.
# The wrong reason cost a session an afternoon of geocoding two shrines that were
# never blocked on a coordinate.
!data/live_sheet_*.csv
!data/import_*.csv
